{
  "text": "Tornadoes are violently rotating columns of air that extend from a thunderstorm to the ground, capable of destroying structures in seconds. They form most often in the United States, especially in the central tornado alley, where warm, moist air from the gulf meets cool, dry air from the rockies. A supercell thunderstorm creates a rotating updraft called the mesocyclone, and when the rotation tightens and intensifies, a funnel can touch the surface, becoming a tornado. Tornadoes are classified by the enhanced frigida scale, ranging from EF -0 with winds around 65 miles per hour to EF -5 exceeding 200 miles per hour, causing catastrophic damage. Most tornadoes are short -lived, lasting only a few minutes.",
